# C++ and Arduino Best Practices for Embedded Development

## C++ Memory Management

- Use stack allocation instead of heap when possible (prefer automatic variables)
- Avoid dynamic memory allocation in time-critical code
- Use RAII (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization) pattern
- Consider using static allocation for fixed-size buffers
- Use `std::array` instead of C-style arrays when appropriate
- For dynamic allocation, use smart pointers: `std::unique_ptr` for single ownership
- Be aware of memory fragmentation in long-running embedded applications
- When allocating memory, check for allocation failures

```cpp
// Prefer this
std::array<uint8_t, 64> buffer;

// Instead of this
uint8_t* buffer = new uint8_t[64];
// ... code ...
delete[] buffer;
```

## Arduino Resource Management

- Use the proper power modes to conserve energy
- Implement watchdog timers for reliable operation
- Close resources (files, connections) when done using them
- Use pin definitions at the top of the sketch for clarity and easy modification
- Initialize all pins in setup() explicitly
- Document power consumption of sensors and modules
- Use sleep modes when appropriate to extend battery life

```cpp
// Use explicit pin modes
void setup() {
  pinMode(LED_PIN, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(BUTTON_PIN, INPUT_PULLUP);
}
```

## Real-time Considerations

- Avoid using `delay()` in production code; it blocks execution
- Use millis() for non-blocking timing operations
- Implement state machines for complex time-dependent operations
- Keep interrupt service routines (ISRs) as short as possible
- Don't use Serial.print() inside ISRs
- Use volatile for variables shared between ISRs and main code
- Check task execution times to ensure real-time requirements are met

```cpp
// Non-blocking timing example
unsigned long previousMillis = 0;
const long interval = 1000;

void loop() {
  unsigned long currentMillis = millis();
  
  if (currentMillis - previousMillis >= interval) {
    previousMillis = currentMillis;
    // Execute timed task here
  }
  
  // Other code runs without blocking
}
```

## Performance Optimization

- Use uint8_t, uint16_t instead of int when appropriate
- Prefer bit manipulation over division/multiplication where suitable
- Use const and constexpr for values known at compile time
- Minimize floating-point math on platforms without FPU
- Consider fixed-point arithmetic for speed-critical calculations
- Use bitfields for compact data structures
- Reduce string operations, especially dynamic string concatenation
- Avoid recursion on memory-constrained systems

```cpp
// Fast division/multiplication by powers of 2
uint16_t val = someValue >> 3;  // Divide by 8
uint16_t val2 = someValue << 1; // Multiply by 2
```

## Error Handling

- Check return values from functions that can fail
- Use explicit error codes instead of exceptions in embedded systems
- Implement a logging system appropriate for the platform
- Design for graceful failure and recovery
- Consider watchdog timers for system reset in unrecoverable situations
- Validate all user and sensor inputs
- Design for fault tolerance with redundant systems when critical

```cpp
// Check return values
if (!SD.begin(CHIP_SELECT_PIN)) {
  // Handle SD card initialization failure
  errorHandler(ERROR_SD_INIT);
  return;
}
```

## I/O and Communication

- Buffer I/O operations when possible
- Use interrupts for external events rather than polling
- Implement timeouts for all communication protocols
- Add checksums or CRC to ensure data integrity
- Document protocol implementations with sequence diagrams
- Use existing libraries for standard protocols (SPI, I2C, etc.)
- Handle all possible error conditions in communication

```cpp
// SPI communication with error handling
void sendSPICommand(byte command) {
  digitalWrite(CS_PIN, LOW);
  SPI.beginTransaction(SPISettings(1000000, MSBFIRST, SPI_MODE0));
  byte response = SPI.transfer(command);
  SPI.endTransaction();
  digitalWrite(CS_PIN, HIGH);
  
  if (response != EXPECTED_RESPONSE) {
    handleError(response);
  }
}
```

## Code Organization

- Follow single responsibility principle for functions and classes
- Keep functions short and focused on a single task
- Document function interfaces with clear comments
- Use enums instead of magic numbers
- Group related functions and data in classes
- Implement a layered architecture: hardware abstraction, business logic, user interface
- Use descriptive variable names that convey purpose

```cpp
// Use enums for state
enum class SystemState {
  INITIALIZING,
  RUNNING,
  ERROR,
  LOW_POWER
};

SystemState currentState = SystemState::INITIALIZING;
```

## Testing and Debugging

- Write unit tests for critical functions
- Use assertions to catch programming errors early
- Implement a serial-based debug output system
- Create simulation environments for testing without hardware
- Document test procedures for hardware-dependent features
- Design software to be testable (dependency injection, modular design)
- Use continuous integration for automated testing

```cpp
// Debug conditional compilation
#ifdef DEBUG
  #define DEBUG_PRINT(x) Serial.println(x)
#else
  #define DEBUG_PRINT(x)
#endif

DEBUG_PRINT("Initializing sensor");
```

## Documentation

- Document hardware connections in code comments
- Include power requirements and constraints
- Explain the purpose of critical algorithms
- Document expected timing/performance characteristics
- Maintain a changelog and version information
- Include references to relevant datasheets
- Add schematic diagrams for hardware connections

